  "VERDICT": {
    "conclusion": "PRE-ASYMPTOTIC sqrt(Q)-oscillation envelope (function-field prediction CONFIRMED)",
    "why": [
      "Windowed exponent delta([Q,4Q]) is NOT stable near 0.63: it swings 0.50->1.71->0.33 across the sweep, for all three candidate C. A genuine power law would give a flat delta.",
      "R(Q)*sqrt(Q) does NOT decay to 0: range [-0.86,+0.90], |last|/|first|~1.35-1.41>1. R itself changes sign 7 times, migrating from ~-0.02 (Q=1k) through 0 to +0.0056 (Q=26k). This is the O(1) oscillation signature of a true Q^{-1/2} decay modulated by zeta-zero terms, NOT a single exponent >1/2.",
      "Toy explicit-formula model with TRUE exponent exactly 0.5 yields single-power delta_eff mean 0.53 but std 0.25, range [0.11,1.0] across realizations: a sqrt(Q)-oscillation envelope, fit over a finite range, readily fakes a single exponent anywhere incl ~0.63."
    ],
    "robust_to_C": "Identical conclusion for twin-prime/2, A065483, and data-estimated C (they differ by ~1e-4 but drift/oscillation pattern is unchanged).",
    "caveat": "Q range 1k-26k via exact O(Q^2) enumeration. To DECISIVELY pin the limit one would push exact NW to Q~1e5-1e6 (needs an O(Q log Q) NW algorithm, not this enumerator) and check R*sqrt(Q) stays O(1) without secular decay; current data already shows NO decay of R*sqrt(Q), which is the key prediction."
  }
